WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.100
CI CD.

2
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:05.612
The best teams deploy many times a day and barely notice.

3
00:00:05.612 --> 00:00:07.133
Here is how they get there.

4
00:00:08.333 --> 00:00:09.567
Start with the culture.

5
00:00:09.867 --> 00:00:14.300
A team that deploys twenty times a day ships small, low-risk changes.

6
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:18.133
A team that deploys quarterly ships giant, terrifying ones.

7
00:00:18.433 --> 00:00:20.567
Continuous means small and reversible.

8
00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:29.800
Continuous integration means every push is built and tested automatically, so broken code is caught in minutes, at the pull request.

9
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:34.733
Continuous delivery means deploys are automated, reversible and routine.

10
00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:37.438
Delivery means every change can ship.

11
00:00:37.438 --> 00:00:39.712
Deployment means every change does.

12
00:00:39.712 --> 00:00:42.767
Most teams keep a human click on the last step.

13
00:00:43.967 --> 00:00:45.767
Here is a six-stage pipeline.

14
00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:46.967
Lint the code.

15
00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:48.300
Run the tests.

16
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:49.633
Build the image.

17
00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:51.500
Scan for vulnerabilities.

18
00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.867
Deploy to staging, then production.

19
00:00:54.167 --> 00:00:56.046
And verify with a smoke test.

20
00:00:56.046 --> 00:00:58.767
Each stage must pass before the next runs.

21
00:00:59.967 --> 00:01:02.900
Every stage needs a defined failure behavior.

22
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.938
Lint, test and build failures block the merge.

23
00:01:05.938 --> 00:01:08.200
You fix it with a commit, not a retry.

24
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.100
A scan with critical vulnerabilities blocks too.

25
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:15.067
A failed deploy rolls back to the last good image automatically.

26
00:01:15.367 --> 00:01:17.900
Only a failed smoke test pages a human.

27
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:20.767
Here is the transformation.

28
00:01:21.067 --> 00:01:26.805
Early deploys were a Friday evening ritual: ssh in, git pull, restart, and pray.

29
00:01:26.805 --> 00:01:31.467
Once, a broken retriever shipped and nobody noticed until Monday.

30
00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:35.333
With the pipeline, a deploy is just merging the pull request.

31
00:01:35.633 --> 00:01:40.074
That class of bug now dies at stage two, in CI, minutes after the push.

32
00:01:40.074 --> 00:01:45.767
You can deploy on a Friday at five without a second thought, because rollback is automatic.

33
00:01:46.967 --> 00:01:47.851
Let's recap.

34
00:01:47.851 --> 00:01:51.533
CI tests every push, and CD makes deploys routine.

35
00:01:51.833 --> 00:01:54.033
A pipeline is a set of ordered gates.

36
00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:57.133
Every stage has a defined failure behavior.

37
00:01:57.433 --> 00:01:59.806
And reversibility beats perfection.

38
00:01:59.806 --> 00:02:01.433
Next up: GitHub Actions.
